{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue128;}{\fonttbl{\f0
\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\b 2}  We can gain some 
information simply by examining the plots as we make repeated runs by 
enclosing the equation generating the noise sample and pushing F9 repeatedly.}
.TXT 6 0 218 0 0
Cg a74.000000,74.000000,486
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue128;}{\fonttbl{\f0
\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\b 3}  We would like to 
consider the question of the "best" M. We need to define "best": we do 
this by setting up a {\b criterion} which will be minimized by choice 
of M. We choose the {\b mean-square error criterion} for a number of 
reasons: it is mathematically tractable - we can solve for the best M 
often, it is a common choice, it is a reasonable criterion when we 
want a good copy of the signal, and it is a {\b robust} criterion - 
that is, it often seems to work well - e.g., it is practical, etc.}
.TXT 14 0 266 0 0
Cg a57.125000,57.125000,28
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue128;}{\fonttbl{\f0
\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}{\f1\fcharset0\fnil Times New Roman;}}\plain\cf1
\fs20 \pard {\f1\b time-averaged\par squared error:}}
.EQN 0 18 267 0 0
{0:mse1}NAME:(1)/({0:N}NAME)*((0,{0:N}NAME-1,{0:j}NAME,((({0:j}NAME<{0:L}NAME)*({0:s}NAME)[({0:j}NAME)-({0:y}NAME)[({0:j}NAME)))^(2)){64})
.EQN 0 28 268 0 0
{0:mse1}NAME={0}?_n_u_l_l_
.TXT 8 -46 224 0 0
Cg a57.125000,57.125000,418
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue128;}{\fonttbl{\f0
\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\b 4}  If we make many 
trials computing the mean-square error, we see that, generally there 
is a variablilty in mse1 - after all, it is a {\b random variable}. 
This is a definite defect in a criterion - we would like a {\b number}
.This is done in theory by averaging over the distribution of the 
noise: this is called an {\b ensemble average} - versus the{\b  time average} 
formed above. This computation can be done here but it is not easy! }
.TXT 15 0 226 0 0
Cg a70.375000,70.375000,380
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue128;}{\fonttbl{\f0
\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard We use a method commonly 
employed to approximate the ensemble average of the time-averaged 
squared error by forming the sample average of many trials. ("many" 
depends, in part, on Mathcad and the computer!) We can do this using 
modifying the above staements to generate an indexed set of mse s 
which we can then average. We basically index the noise sample 
sequences as shown now.}
.TXT 10 -4 335 0 0
C x1,1,0,0
.TXT 2 4 247 0 0
